{"qid":"Q83958","names":["Macedonian Empire"],"title":"Macedonian Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The ancient kingdom of Macedon, attested in our dataset from c. 675 BC, rose from a peripheral Greek-speaking monarchy to domination of the Aegean under Philip II (r. 359–336 BC), who welded the fragmented poleis into a Macedonian-led league. Under his son Alexander III (r. 336–323 BC), the kingdom conquered the Achaemenid Persian Empire, extending Macedonian rule from Egypt to the Punjab and founding dozens of Alexandria-named cities along the route. Alexander's death in Babylon in 323 BC triggered the Wars of the Diadochi; by the 290s BC the original Macedonian heartland had settled under the Antigonid dynasty, while the broader Macedonian empire had fractured into successor kingdoms, ending the unified polity our dataset closes at c. 292 BC.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The Macedonian heartland is well-attested from administrative, numismatic, and epigraphic sources; Alexander's campaign routes are documented through literary and archaeological evidence, but the outer margins of control in Central Asia and India are reconstructed from campaign narratives rather than boundary inscriptions and carry moderate uncertainty.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Alexander the Great","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Alexander-the-Great"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Macedonia (ancient kingdom)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macedonia_(ancient_kingdom)"}],"britannica":"place/Macedonia-ancient-kingdom-Europe","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Macedonia (ancient kingdom)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"nam